WEBVTT

00:00.800 --> 00:12.333 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
[Bright music]

00:12.333 --> 00:15.666 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>One million neural
connections per second.

00:15.666 --> 00:17.733 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
That's the speed at
which brain science

00:17.733 --> 00:19.366 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
clocks a child's brain

00:19.366 --> 00:21.900 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
developing between
birth and age three.

00:21.900 --> 00:23.433 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
And science shows

00:23.433 --> 00:25.933 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that early nurturing from
parents and caregivers

00:25.933 --> 00:28.400 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
is vital to boosting
those neural connections

00:28.400 --> 00:29.633 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
and budding brains

00:29.633 --> 00:31.200 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
and to equipping babies

00:31.200 --> 00:34.000 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
with the sensory pathways
and brainy bedrock

00:34.000 --> 00:36.100 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
they'll need to succeed in life.

00:36.100 --> 00:39.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Yet, while every child needs
early parental involvement,

00:39.700 --> 00:42.333 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
for kids with learning
and attention issues,

00:42.333 --> 00:44.766 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
early intervention
stands as perhaps

00:44.766 --> 00:47.933 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
the most consequential
thing parents can do

00:47.933 --> 00:51.266 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
to ensure their child
reaches their full potential.

00:51.266 --> 00:55.300 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Early intervention primarily
describes services and support

00:55.300 --> 00:57.466 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
children from birth to age 3

00:57.466 --> 01:00.133 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
with learning and
attention issues received

01:00.133 --> 01:02.866 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that address their
individual communication,

01:02.866 --> 01:05.800 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
social, emotional,
and learning needs,

01:05.800 --> 01:07.433 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
helps them to reach their peak,

01:07.433 --> 01:09.566 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
and can reduce the likelihood

01:09.566 --> 01:11.100 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
the child will experience

01:11.100 --> 01:14.166 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
more serious mental health
problems down the line.

01:15.300 --> 01:18.233 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
On this episode, we
visit a Texas school

01:18.233 --> 01:19.933 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
for neurodivergent students

01:19.933 --> 01:23.133 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that finds fun ways to
tackle the serious task

01:23.133 --> 01:25.300 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
of early intervention.

01:25.300 --> 01:27.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Next, our panel of
national experts

01:27.533 --> 01:29.833 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
discusses how early intervention

01:29.833 --> 01:32.900 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
can enhance children's outcomes.

01:32.900 --> 01:35.566 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Later, you'll meet our
latest difference maker,

01:35.566 --> 01:37.633 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
a renowned British academic

01:37.633 --> 01:41.233 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
with much to say about
equity in higher education

01:41.233 --> 01:46.033 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
who through childhood,
negotiated silence in autism.

01:46.033 --> 01:49.366 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
First, we traveled to
the Joy School in Houston

01:49.366 --> 01:51.000 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
where we learned that sometimes

01:51.000 --> 01:54.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
the answers to early
intervention with the three Rs

01:54.533 --> 01:58.600 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
means getting your
hands dirty, literally.

01:58.600 --> 02:02.600 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Chief correspondent Cindy
Peterson brings us the story.

02:08.000 --> 02:09.233 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
>>It isn't unusual

02:09.233 --> 02:11.133 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
for parents to burst
into the bathroom

02:11.133 --> 02:14.033 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
and find their little ones
playing with dad's shaving cream

02:14.033 --> 02:16.133 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
for a bit of mischievous fun.

02:16.133 --> 02:18.633 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
But at the Joy School
in Houston, Texas,

02:18.633 --> 02:21.466 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
elementary students are
lathering up the Barbasol

02:21.466 --> 02:23.300 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
for a greater purpose.

02:23.300 --> 02:26.033 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
>>Reading is a rope and there
are lots of different layers

02:26.033 --> 02:27.566 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
and things that the
kids need to learn.

02:27.566 --> 02:30.366 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
And so, focusing on those
early foundational skills

02:30.366 --> 02:32.500 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
like the phonics, the
phonemic awareness piece,

02:32.500 --> 02:36.033 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and then getting them
to blend and decode

02:36.033 --> 02:37.833 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
and encode to that spelling.

02:37.833 --> 02:40.433 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And then, but also we do talk
a lot about the comprehension.

02:40.433 --> 02:43.566 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, reading books,
doing a think aloud.

02:43.566 --> 02:46.366 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, if I read a story,
retelling the story,

02:46.366 --> 02:48.766 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
talking about what I think the
characters might be feeling,

02:48.766 --> 02:50.766 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and then having the kids
share their thinking,

02:50.766 --> 02:52.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
really lays again,
a solid foundation

02:52.700 --> 02:54.500 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
for them as they get older.

02:54.500 --> 02:56.566 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>This is just one
of the tools in the toolbox

02:56.566 --> 02:59.033 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that teachers at this
private school for children

02:59.033 --> 03:01.100 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
with learning and
attention differences

03:01.100 --> 03:03.166 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
help support and
equip their students

03:03.166 --> 03:04.766 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
through early intervention.

03:04.766 --> 03:06.466 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>One of the biggest
things that we do

03:06.466 --> 03:08.900 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
is we teach explicitly.

03:08.900 --> 03:10.433 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
And so,

03:10.433 --> 03:11.933 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
we're not just assuming

03:11.933 --> 03:14.033 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that a child is going to
learn to read by osmosis,

03:14.033 --> 03:15.833 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
we know that they
need systematic,

03:15.833 --> 03:17.966 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
detailed instruction,

03:17.966 --> 03:20.766 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
explicit instruction
in how to read

03:20.766 --> 03:22.900 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and what the rules
are for reading.

03:22.900 --> 03:25.433 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And that makes all the
difference in the world.

03:25.433 --> 03:27.000 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>Early
intervention programs

03:27.000 --> 03:29.333 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
are designed to enhance
the learning abilities

03:29.333 --> 03:31.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and functional
skills of these kids,

03:31.533 --> 03:34.500 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
boosting their self-esteem
and improving communication,

03:34.500 --> 03:36.933 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
social interaction,
self-regulation,

03:36.933 --> 03:38.266 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
problem solving,

03:38.266 --> 03:42.100 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and other critical skills
necessary for success.

03:42.100 --> 03:45.733 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
>>One of the
commonalities is that

03:45.733 --> 03:48.966 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
these are the kiddos
who don't necessarily

03:50.133 --> 03:51.766 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
pick up on things intuitively.

03:51.766 --> 03:54.933 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, what might come
naturally to other kids

03:54.933 --> 03:57.466 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
has to be more directly
taught with our kids.

03:57.466 --> 04:02.033 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, the earlier we can
teach them how to learn,

04:02.033 --> 04:03.233 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
how their brain works,

04:03.233 --> 04:06.433 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
how to know if they're
paying attention,

04:06.433 --> 04:09.133 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
how to know if they are on task,

04:09.133 --> 04:12.600 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
how to know when
they do need help,

04:12.600 --> 04:16.266 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and how to specifically hone
in on where they need help,

04:17.633 --> 04:20.133 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
the more beneficial that's
going to be in the long run.

04:20.133 --> 04:22.433 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>There's some
statistics that say,

04:22.433 --> 04:23.833 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
if you don't provide
intervention

04:23.833 --> 04:25.200 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
before the age of eight,

04:25.200 --> 04:27.466 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
they're likely to struggle with
reading through high school

04:27.466 --> 04:30.233 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
like, it's a 75% chance.

04:30.233 --> 04:34.033 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, when you can
help remediate those,

04:34.033 --> 04:36.900 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
specifically in the phonological
component of the brain

04:36.900 --> 04:38.300 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
when you can remediate

04:39.100 --> 04:41.666 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
and teach them in kindergarten,

04:41.666 --> 04:46.733 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
then their likelihood of not
having significant struggles

04:47.533 --> 04:49.100 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
throughout is much higher.

04:49.100 --> 04:51.666 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>Oftentimes, a low
self-esteem or self-image

04:51.666 --> 04:53.033 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
clouds a child's mind

04:53.033 --> 04:54.933 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
when they struggle
with basic learning.

04:54.933 --> 04:56.200 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
That's why the Joy School

04:56.200 --> 04:59.366 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
aims to not only provide
strategies for success,

04:59.366 --> 05:01.633 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
but promote an
environment of positivity,

05:01.633 --> 05:04.566 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
celebrating each child's
individual growth.

05:04.566 --> 05:06.433 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>The longer kids
struggle in school,

05:06.433 --> 05:08.966 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
whether that's
academically or socially,

05:08.966 --> 05:12.033 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
the more their
self-esteem is impacted

05:12.033 --> 05:14.933 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and that's what
takes longer to fix.

05:14.933 --> 05:18.033 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
There are all kinds of
scientifically backed

05:18.033 --> 05:20.833 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
methods for teaching
reading or teaching math

05:20.833 --> 05:22.466 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
for kids who struggle
in those areas,

05:22.466 --> 05:27.100 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
but reclaiming a
child's self-worth

05:27.100 --> 05:30.233 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and helping them realize
that they are capable,

05:30.233 --> 05:32.933 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
despite whatever learning
differences they may have,

05:32.933 --> 05:34.266 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
that takes a lot longer.

05:34.266 --> 05:36.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>Adults like to say
that kids are resilient

05:36.533 --> 05:39.033 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
but they're also very observant

05:39.033 --> 05:40.833 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
and they're often big feelers

05:40.833 --> 05:42.933 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and school is like
their occupation.

05:42.933 --> 05:46.166 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And so, when they feel like
they're not good at it,

05:46.166 --> 05:47.800 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
the impact that it can have

05:47.800 --> 05:52.866 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
on how they feel as
a person can be huge.

05:53.866 --> 05:55.266 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
>>As a transitional program,

05:55.266 --> 05:57.266 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
the Joy School has
seen great success

05:57.266 --> 06:00.766 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
in acclimating kids back into
public or private schools

06:00.766 --> 06:04.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
after equipping them with the
tools they need to succeed.

06:04.700 --> 06:07.366 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>On average, our kids are
here three to four years

06:07.366 --> 06:10.200 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
and so, we've shown repeatedly

06:10.200 --> 06:14.333 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that this little
detour where we can,

06:14.333 --> 06:16.166 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
you know, give kids
some breathing room

06:16.166 --> 06:19.433 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and take a detour off
the standardized path

06:19.433 --> 06:21.200 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
of what they're supposed
to be able to do

06:21.200 --> 06:23.333 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
just because they're
in X grade level

06:24.200 --> 06:26.366 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
is really, really effective.

06:26.366 --> 06:29.333 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>Max, who had no
self-confidence,

06:29.333 --> 06:32.500 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
didn't wanna leave the house,
didn't wanna go on play dates,

06:32.500 --> 06:34.200 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
didn't want to try new things,

06:36.933 --> 06:38.200 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
stuck to himself

06:38.200 --> 06:42.666 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
to now outgoing, willing
to do new things,

06:42.666 --> 06:44.733 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
willing to try different things

06:44.733 --> 06:46.666 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that are outside of
his comfort zone,

06:47.866 --> 06:50.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and then excited,
more importantly.

06:50.533 --> 06:53.200 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Just the overall excitement
about coming to school

06:53.200 --> 06:56.233 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and sharing his
experiences at school

06:56.233 --> 06:57.700 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
and showing what he's learning,

06:57.700 --> 07:00.100 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
showing that he can do
the math that he can read.

07:01.100 --> 07:03.833 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Where before everything
was a struggle.

07:03.833 --> 07:05.866 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
>>More than anything
with both of my kids,

07:05.866 --> 07:08.566 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
the skills that they
were taught here,

07:09.633 --> 07:12.033 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
they know how to
advocate for themselves.

07:12.033 --> 07:14.900 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
They appreciate who they are,

07:14.900 --> 07:17.666 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
they have a ton of
self-confidence,

07:17.666 --> 07:19.466 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
and I think that that gift

07:19.466 --> 07:22.133 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that they both learned
at an early age

07:22.133 --> 07:24.200 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
is gonna serve them
well, not only in school,

07:24.200 --> 07:27.833 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
but I think it's gonna serve
them throughout their careers.

07:27.833 --> 07:30.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>For A World of
Difference, I'm Cindy Peterson.

07:35.766 --> 07:38.166 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>Next, our expert panel
takes a closer look

07:38.166 --> 07:39.433 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
at early intervention

07:39.433 --> 07:42.300 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and offers a game plan
for parents to follow.

07:48.233 --> 07:50.666 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
Dr. Oksana Hagerty
is an educational

07:50.666 --> 07:52.300 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
and developmental psychologist

07:52.300 --> 07:54.266 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
who serves as a
learning specialist

07:54.266 --> 07:56.566 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
and the dean of the
Center for Student Success

07:56.566 --> 07:58.733 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
at Beacon College in
Leesburg, Florida,

07:58.733 --> 08:01.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
the nation's first accredited
college or university

08:01.700 --> 08:04.366 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
dedicated to educating
neurodiverse students

08:04.366 --> 08:07.300 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
with learning disabilities,
ADHD, dyslexia,

08:07.300 --> 08:09.233 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
and other learning differences.

08:09.233 --> 08:11.933 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Hagerty specializes
in academic support,

08:11.933 --> 08:15.600 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
cognitive abilities, learning
disability interventions,

08:15.600 --> 08:18.900 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and educational and
developmental psychology.

08:18.900 --> 08:22.633 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
Dr. Emily Knight, MD and PhD
is an assistant professor

08:22.633 --> 08:26.033 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
of developmental behavioral
pediatrics and neuroscience

08:26.033 --> 08:28.500 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
at the University of
Rochester Medical Center

08:28.500 --> 08:30.366 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
in Rochester, New York.

08:30.366 --> 08:32.033 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
Dr. Knight's clinical focus

08:32.033 --> 08:35.400 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
is providing patient-centered
subspecialty care

08:35.400 --> 08:38.366 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
for children and adolescents
with a wide range

08:38.366 --> 08:40.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
of neurodevelopmental
disabilities.

08:40.533 --> 08:42.200 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
She also conducts research

08:42.200 --> 08:44.566 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that aims to understand
neuro mechanisms

08:44.566 --> 08:46.266 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
of sensory perception

08:46.266 --> 08:48.966 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
in individuals on
the autism spectrum.

08:50.233 --> 08:53.300 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
Dr. Kimberly Mosca
is head of school

08:53.300 --> 08:55.900 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
at Tribeca Preparatory School,

08:55.900 --> 09:00.000 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
a 12 month independent special
education early childhood

09:00.000 --> 09:02.066 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and elementary
school in New York

09:02.066 --> 09:03.666 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
that prepares their students

09:03.666 --> 09:05.900 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
for a less restrictive
environment

09:05.900 --> 09:09.133 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
by developing their
academic and social skills.

09:09.133 --> 09:12.366 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Mosca is a licensed
behavior analyst,

09:12.366 --> 09:16.033 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
a board certified
behavior analyst doctoral,

09:16.033 --> 09:18.200 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and a certified
special education

09:18.200 --> 09:21.233 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
and general education teacher.

09:21.233 --> 09:25.566 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And we're gonna start our
conversation with Dr. Hagerty.

09:25.566 --> 09:27.466 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
What is early intervention

09:27.466 --> 09:30.600 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and how can it benefit
neurodivergent children?

09:31.733 --> 09:33.800 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>In this strictest
sense of this word,

09:33.800 --> 09:36.866 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
early interventions are
support and services

09:36.866 --> 09:41.000 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
that are provided to children
under the age of three

09:41.000 --> 09:43.366 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
and this can be speech
therapy or physical therapy

09:43.366 --> 09:46.000 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
or any other supports
that are needed

09:46.000 --> 09:47.366 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
to the children and families

09:47.366 --> 09:49.900 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
with neurodevelopmental
disabilities.

09:49.900 --> 09:52.866 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
However, and I work for college

09:52.866 --> 09:55.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and it may be a very
interesting combination

09:55.533 --> 09:57.600 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that I'm here to talk
about early interventions

09:57.600 --> 09:59.333 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
but I wanted to expand
the knowledge of,

09:59.333 --> 10:02.266 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
the concept of early
interventions to just say that

10:02.266 --> 10:04.466 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
early intervention would
be any intervention

10:04.466 --> 10:05.933 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
that is done during the critical

10:05.933 --> 10:07.433 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
or sensitive period of time.

10:07.433 --> 10:10.133 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Because when we talk about,
for example, college students,

10:10.133 --> 10:11.866 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
we do see that those students

10:11.866 --> 10:13.366 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
who didn't have
reading interventions

10:13.366 --> 10:16.866 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
during their first or second
grade in elementary school

10:16.866 --> 10:18.333 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
are lagging behind.

10:18.333 --> 10:20.966 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, to me, if we're talking
about the stricter sense

10:20.966 --> 10:22.500 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
of the word early interventions

10:22.500 --> 10:25.200 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
or interventions done
before the age of three.

10:25.200 --> 10:26.800 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
In the broadest
sense of this word,

10:26.800 --> 10:28.966 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
these are any
interventions that are done

10:28.966 --> 10:31.500 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
during sensitive or
critical periods of time

10:31.500 --> 10:32.966 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
of the development

10:32.966 --> 10:34.500 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and it can be
reading interventions

10:34.500 --> 10:36.800 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
or social skills interventions,

10:36.800 --> 10:40.833 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
can be any interventions done
between the birth of the child

10:40.833 --> 10:42.866 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
to the probably
late adolescence,

10:42.866 --> 10:46.666 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
if we're talking about
executive functions.

10:46.666 --> 10:47.966 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
>>Thank you, Dr. Hagerty.

10:47.966 --> 10:51.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, Dr. Knight, what is
the most recent research

10:51.533 --> 10:54.200 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
showing about the efficacy
of early intervention

10:54.200 --> 10:56.633 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
for neuro divergent students?

10:56.633 --> 10:58.500 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
>>Yeah, so we know from research

10:58.500 --> 11:02.133 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that early childhood is a period
where the brain is growing

11:02.133 --> 11:04.833 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
and has really enormous
capacity for change.

11:04.833 --> 11:06.933 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
And so, what we have is
a wonderful opportunity

11:06.933 --> 11:08.433 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
to support that brain
development

11:08.433 --> 11:10.533 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
with early interventions.

11:10.533 --> 11:13.733 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And what our research
is showing is that

11:13.733 --> 11:15.600 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
targeted early interventions

11:15.600 --> 11:19.433 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
are associated with improvements
in some really key areas

11:19.433 --> 11:21.333 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
for neurodivergent children.

11:21.333 --> 11:24.333 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
For example, we may
see improvements in

11:24.333 --> 11:29.100 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
language, play, joint attention,
and daily living function.

11:29.100 --> 11:31.633 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
And as Dr. Hagerty referenced,

11:31.633 --> 11:34.000 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
even beyond the
child themselves,

11:34.000 --> 11:36.233 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
early intervention
plays an important role

11:36.233 --> 11:38.633 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
in helping support the
whole family and parents.

11:38.633 --> 11:42.200 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And so, when families are
connected to interventions

11:42.200 --> 11:44.966 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and feel empowered to
advocate for services,

11:44.966 --> 11:47.233 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
they report that
they feel less stress

11:47.233 --> 11:48.466 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
and they feel better equipped

11:48.466 --> 11:50.266 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
to support their
child's development.

11:51.266 --> 11:52.100 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
>>Thank you.

11:52.100 --> 11:53.500 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
So, Dr. Mosca,

11:53.500 --> 11:55.800 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
what are some of the
learning and attention issues

11:55.800 --> 11:58.233 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that these neurodivergent
children are facing

11:58.233 --> 12:00.566 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that would require
early intervention?

12:01.666 --> 12:03.400 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>That's a really
great question.

12:03.400 --> 12:07.033 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
A lot of the early
signs are things

12:07.033 --> 12:08.500 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that we might not
really think of.

12:08.500 --> 12:10.833 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
So, a lot of times there are,

12:10.833 --> 12:13.233 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
you know, in the
past couple years,

12:13.233 --> 12:14.666 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
there's been things
you might think of,

12:14.666 --> 12:16.066 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
lack of eye contact

12:16.066 --> 12:19.766 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
or maybe repetitive movements
such as hand flapping.

12:19.766 --> 12:24.833 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
But there's really three
areas that are very common

12:25.866 --> 12:28.600 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
in developmental
delays that signal us

12:28.600 --> 12:31.133 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that a child likely
needs some intervention.

12:31.133 --> 12:34.366 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, the first area is in
the area of communication.

12:34.366 --> 12:38.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, that can really span
from receptive language

12:38.700 --> 12:41.566 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
of not being able to
respond to one's name

12:41.566 --> 12:44.766 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
to even further
on in development

12:44.766 --> 12:48.866 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
in the lack of ability
to read social cues

12:48.866 --> 12:52.366 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
and be able to change topics.

12:52.366 --> 12:54.733 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, some of our families
have a hard time

12:54.733 --> 12:56.566 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
thinking that there is
a communication issue

12:56.566 --> 12:58.066 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
because they'll say,

12:58.066 --> 13:00.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
well, Billy can speak
about dinosaurs for hours

13:00.700 --> 13:04.900 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
but that is an issue in
pragmatic language development.

13:04.900 --> 13:07.066 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And some of these children
actually are speaking

13:07.066 --> 13:08.633 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
before the age of three.

13:08.633 --> 13:11.366 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And that is an area
that is very difficult

13:11.366 --> 13:13.133 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
for families to often identify

13:13.133 --> 13:18.000 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
because they might actually
have a pretty robust vocabulary.

13:18.000 --> 13:22.266 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
The children that have a
little bit more obvious signs

13:22.266 --> 13:24.566 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
of not being able to
respond to their name,

13:24.566 --> 13:28.733 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
follow directions, or say
their wants and needs,

13:28.733 --> 13:31.800 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
those are signs that
parents typically see

13:31.800 --> 13:33.500 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
a little bit earlier.

13:33.500 --> 13:36.833 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Another area is with
frustration tolerance.

13:36.833 --> 13:40.200 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, children tend to
have a harder time

13:40.200 --> 13:41.700 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
soothing themselves.

13:41.700 --> 13:45.466 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And parents notice that the
tantrums might last longer

13:45.466 --> 13:47.033 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
if the child doesn't
get something

13:47.033 --> 13:48.600 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
just the way they want it.

13:48.600 --> 13:51.166 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And oftentimes it seems kind
of like, a strange reason.

13:51.166 --> 13:53.466 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
So, it might be,

13:53.466 --> 13:55.600 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
parents are asking themselves
I just don't understand,

13:55.600 --> 13:58.500 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
why is my child upset?

13:58.500 --> 14:01.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
With that being said, they
might also have difficulties

14:01.700 --> 14:03.600 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
being soothed by themselves,

14:03.600 --> 14:05.933 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
so self soothing
or by a caretaker.

14:05.933 --> 14:07.866 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, a lot of times as
parents were taught,

14:07.866 --> 14:09.900 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
you know, to rub
the back or a coup

14:09.900 --> 14:12.666 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
or do things to make
them or children calm.

14:12.666 --> 14:14.766 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
But a lot of times
these children

14:14.766 --> 14:16.733 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
aren't able to calm themselves

14:16.733 --> 14:21.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
with those typical kind of
ways of calming a child.

14:21.700 --> 14:24.133 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And the last area,
which is really

14:24.133 --> 14:27.033 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
is a sign that
children do need help

14:27.033 --> 14:31.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
is the delayed acquisition
of daily living skills.

14:31.533 --> 14:34.266 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
So, that can include toileting,

14:34.266 --> 14:37.466 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
brushing, teeth,
eating, dressing.

14:37.466 --> 14:39.266 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, these skills
are typically skills

14:39.266 --> 14:41.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that children learn
through imitation,

14:41.533 --> 14:43.766 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
imitating their caretakers.

14:43.766 --> 14:46.033 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
At a certain age you hear

14:46.033 --> 14:48.933 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that a child wants to do
something on their own.

14:48.933 --> 14:50.633 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
So, a lot of times our children

14:50.633 --> 14:52.766 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
might have delays in those areas

14:52.766 --> 14:55.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and those skills have to
be directly taught to them

14:55.700 --> 14:57.833 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
and then repeated.

14:57.833 --> 14:59.300 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
So, those would be, I would say

14:59.300 --> 15:03.100 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
the three big areas that
family should be looking at.

15:03.100 --> 15:04.600 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
>>Thank you.

15:04.600 --> 15:07.033 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, Dr. Hagerty, can you
give us some examples then

15:07.033 --> 15:10.933 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
how early intervention
can help to

15:10.933 --> 15:13.766 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
mitigate and remediate
some of these issues

15:13.766 --> 15:17.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that children who are neuro
divergent might experience

15:17.533 --> 15:20.000 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
during this particular
developmental time?

15:20.800 --> 15:23.366 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
>>In these recent years

15:23.366 --> 15:25.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and since we are
college specializing

15:25.700 --> 15:28.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
on working with students with
learning intentional issues,

15:28.533 --> 15:31.466 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
there has been research that
supports the point of view

15:31.466 --> 15:34.100 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that specific learning
disabilities can develop

15:34.100 --> 15:36.233 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
in response to an
extreme lack of

15:36.233 --> 15:38.666 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
environmental
stimulation early on.

15:38.666 --> 15:43.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, any kind of deprivation
early on will eventually lead,

15:45.166 --> 15:48.566 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
will eventually lead to a
specific learning disability

15:48.566 --> 15:50.633 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
or may lead to a specific
learning disability,

15:50.633 --> 15:54.300 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
especially if other
factors are in place.

15:54.300 --> 15:58.433 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
For example, if I were to talk
about the reading disability,

15:58.433 --> 16:00.866 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
specific reading
disability, I would say that

16:00.866 --> 16:04.600 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
interventions study during
the first or the second grade

16:04.600 --> 16:06.033 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
are most effective.

16:06.033 --> 16:08.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And what they do, they
improve the efficiency

16:08.533 --> 16:11.000 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and automaticity
of reading skills

16:11.000 --> 16:15.200 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that really create the reading
experience for the students

16:15.200 --> 16:18.466 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that prepare them
later on in their life

16:18.466 --> 16:20.833 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
during their teenage
or adolescent years

16:20.833 --> 16:22.066 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
for reading comprehension.

16:22.066 --> 16:24.033 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Because what we are
very often seeing

16:24.033 --> 16:27.666 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
students who were not
taught to read properly

16:27.666 --> 16:29.133 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
during the elementary
school years,

16:29.133 --> 16:31.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
then develop reading
comprehension difficulties,

16:31.533 --> 16:33.800 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
not necessarily because
they lack fluency,

16:33.800 --> 16:36.666 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
but because they lack vocabulary
or background knowledge.

16:36.666 --> 16:39.300 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And if there are no
early, and again,

16:39.300 --> 16:41.633 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
I use the word
early very broadly

16:41.633 --> 16:43.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
because when we talk about
reading comprehension

16:43.700 --> 16:45.500 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
in teenager or adolescent years,

16:45.500 --> 16:48.733 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
we're talking about strategies
which is executive function.

16:48.733 --> 16:51.066 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, if there are no executive
function interventions

16:51.066 --> 16:53.200 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
for students who are reading

16:53.200 --> 16:55.266 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
during ninth, 10th,
11th, 12th grade,

16:55.266 --> 16:57.933 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
they do not develop the
strategist that they need.

16:57.933 --> 17:00.166 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
So, in this sense of the word,

17:00.166 --> 17:02.300 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
we need to really know
the sensitive periods

17:02.300 --> 17:04.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
for the development
of each ability,

17:04.700 --> 17:07.433 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
in this sense, in this
case, academic ability

17:07.433 --> 17:11.433 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and provide interventions that
will create brain structures

17:11.433 --> 17:14.566 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
to support the
acquisition of the skill

17:14.566 --> 17:16.533 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
and the practice of the skill.

17:16.533 --> 17:18.066 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
And this will create

17:18.066 --> 17:20.433 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
a loner who is capable
of performing tasks

17:20.433 --> 17:22.200 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
at an expert level.

17:23.166 --> 17:24.800 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
>>All right. So, Dr. Knight,

17:24.800 --> 17:29.166 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
can you give us a look at what
early intervention looks like

17:29.166 --> 17:33.933 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
as far as helping kids
develop social skills

17:33.933 --> 17:36.833 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and be able to
develop relationships?

17:36.833 --> 17:38.333 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
How does that look,

17:38.333 --> 17:41.366 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
the kinds of processes
involved with that?

17:42.666 --> 17:45.300 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>Yeah, so, social interactions
and forming relationships

17:45.300 --> 17:48.733 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
are actually some of the most
complex and nuance things

17:48.733 --> 17:50.366 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
that we do as humans,

17:50.366 --> 17:52.600 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
even if we aren't really
thinking about it.

17:52.600 --> 17:54.100 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
And so, because of that,

17:54.100 --> 17:57.133 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
early in life, it is
important to establish

17:57.133 --> 18:00.066 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
a strong social skill foundation

18:00.066 --> 18:02.366 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that we can continue
to build on.

18:02.366 --> 18:04.933 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And what early
intervention will do

18:04.933 --> 18:06.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
is meet the child
where they're at

18:06.700 --> 18:09.000 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
and help build from there.

18:09.000 --> 18:12.433 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
These foundational
social skills.

18:13.766 --> 18:15.766 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>Watch the full
Ask the Expert segment

18:15.766 --> 18:19.833 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
on our website at AWODTV.org

18:19.833 --> 18:22.433 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
if you wanna learn
more about this topic.

18:22.433 --> 18:25.666 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
You can also watch or
listen on Facebook, YouTube,

18:25.666 --> 18:28.400 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
or on your favorite
podcasting platform.

18:31.533 --> 18:34.200 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
[Gentle music]

18:36.433 --> 18:39.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Next, let's reveal our
latest difference maker.

18:39.533 --> 18:41.700 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
As the well worn proverb says,

18:41.700 --> 18:44.033 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
"Silence is golden."

18:44.033 --> 18:46.133 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
That is, unless you're
a loving mother,

18:46.133 --> 18:48.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
desperate to hear your
child speak your name

18:48.700 --> 18:53.466 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
or whisper that precious
trio of words, "I love you."

18:53.466 --> 18:56.333 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Unable to speak or write,
Jason Arday for years

18:56.333 --> 18:58.533 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
bore silent witness to the world

18:58.533 --> 19:01.100 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
as he navigated
his autism journey,

19:01.100 --> 19:04.866 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
always observing,
thinking, and learning.

19:04.866 --> 19:07.733 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
When he eventually found
his words as a tweenager,

19:07.733 --> 19:10.433 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Arday was finally free
to share with the world

19:10.433 --> 19:13.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
the intellect that had been
percolating in silence.

19:13.533 --> 19:17.466 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
In the spring of
2023, Dr. Jason Arday

19:17.466 --> 19:19.733 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
became the youngest
black professor ever

19:19.733 --> 19:23.666 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
appointed to the University
of Cambridge in England.

19:23.666 --> 19:27.133 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Now, the once silent
youngster speaks volumes

19:27.133 --> 19:29.733 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
about democratizing
higher education

19:29.733 --> 19:32.166 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
with his work as a sociologist

19:32.166 --> 19:35.766 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that seeks to illuminate the
gaps of underrepresented groups

19:35.766 --> 19:38.800 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
pursuing and delivering it.

19:38.800 --> 19:41.400 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Chief correspondent
Cindy Peterson

19:41.400 --> 19:44.333 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and London based
videographer, Elijah Grant

19:44.333 --> 19:45.966 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
brings us his story.

19:47.066 --> 19:49.733 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
[Gentle music]

19:51.366 --> 19:53.800 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
>>In a world where
barriers often obstruct

19:53.800 --> 19:55.266 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
the path to success,

19:55.266 --> 19:57.833 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
there are individuals
who rise above adversity,

19:57.833 --> 20:01.900 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
defying expectations, and
redefining what is possible.

20:01.900 --> 20:05.266 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Meet Professor Jason Arday,
a remarkable sociologist

20:05.266 --> 20:06.633 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
whose journey to success

20:06.633 --> 20:09.400 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
has been nothing short
of extraordinary.

20:09.400 --> 20:11.633 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Diagnosed at an early
age with autism,

20:11.633 --> 20:13.433 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
he faced significant challenges

20:13.433 --> 20:16.566 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
including the inability
to speak until he was 11

20:16.566 --> 20:19.200 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and the struggle to read
and write until he was 18.

20:20.300 --> 20:22.266 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
>>So, after hundreds
and thousands of hours

20:22.266 --> 20:26.533 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
of speech therapy,
I was very fortunate

20:26.533 --> 20:28.033 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
and I guess I look
at it as fortunate

20:28.033 --> 20:30.600 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
'cause people persevere
with you, including my mom

20:31.733 --> 20:34.133 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
who accompanied me to
all of those sessions

20:34.133 --> 20:35.566 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
and other people.

20:35.566 --> 20:37.733 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
At the age of 11, I
then said my first word,

20:37.733 --> 20:39.000 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
which was, hello.

20:39.000 --> 20:40.766 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, that was quite
a surreal moment

20:40.766 --> 20:42.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
because as my mom
always recalls,

20:42.533 --> 20:44.433 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
I used it in the right context.

20:44.433 --> 20:46.400 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
What people don't
always realize is that

20:46.400 --> 20:48.233 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
subsequently from
the age of 11 to 16,

20:48.233 --> 20:49.766 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
there was still a
lot of learning to do

20:49.766 --> 20:53.033 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
in terms of kind of phonic
exercises, soundings,

20:53.033 --> 20:55.666 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
making sense of when to use
the right kind of words,

20:55.666 --> 20:58.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
tenses and phrases in
just spoken dialogue.

20:58.533 --> 20:59.833 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
It was really when
I got to like 16

20:59.833 --> 21:01.466 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
that I felt more comfortable

21:01.466 --> 21:02.966 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
with kind of the
English vernacular

21:02.966 --> 21:05.600 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
in terms of how to use the
right types of language

21:05.600 --> 21:07.233 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
and how to engage with people.

21:07.233 --> 21:10.300 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
The period of kind of
living non-verbally

21:10.300 --> 21:12.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
to be quite honest,
was a privilege.

21:12.533 --> 21:17.000 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
I got to observe
and absorb the world

21:17.000 --> 21:21.000 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
in a really unique way that
most people don't get to.

21:21.000 --> 21:22.900 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, I got to observe
human interaction.

21:22.900 --> 21:24.666 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
And what I became obsessed with

21:24.666 --> 21:27.600 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
was how human beings
interacted with one another.

21:27.600 --> 21:29.033 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
Little did I know

21:29.033 --> 21:30.600 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
in the kind of
paralysis of speech

21:30.600 --> 21:33.833 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
that I would be observing ways

21:33.833 --> 21:36.800 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
in which I could engage with
human beings when I was older.

21:36.800 --> 21:39.200 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>Jason then decided
to further his education

21:39.200 --> 21:42.033 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and studied to become a
teacher of physical education

21:42.033 --> 21:44.100 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
at the University of Surrey.

21:44.100 --> 21:46.933 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>I didn't, no clue what I
was doing to be quite frank.

21:46.933 --> 21:50.066 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
I didn't know what I was doing.

21:50.066 --> 21:52.666 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Like, you do what you
think is your best.

21:54.233 --> 21:57.200 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
You know, I didn't understand
the language people use

21:57.200 --> 21:58.166 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
when they were lecturing.

21:58.166 --> 22:00.266 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
I didn't understand texts.

22:00.266 --> 22:03.466 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And you gotta remember that
when I went to university,

22:03.466 --> 22:07.266 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
I was 20, but I had the
reading age of a 12 year old.

22:08.466 --> 22:11.666 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And I guess a lot
of what I did was

22:11.666 --> 22:14.600 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
repetition and practice,
repetition and practice.

22:14.600 --> 22:18.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So what I used to do was
write 500 words a day

22:18.700 --> 22:21.766 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
every single day for
basically 15 years.

22:21.766 --> 22:23.266 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
And I practiced and I practiced.

22:23.266 --> 22:25.133 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
So, at university, this
was even more exaggerated.

22:25.133 --> 22:27.900 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
I practiced and I practiced and
I practiced and I practiced.

22:27.900 --> 22:31.600 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And the thing that
kind of was the,

22:31.600 --> 22:35.133 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
you know, that propelled
that way of thinking

22:35.133 --> 22:37.600 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
was really my mum's
kind of endeavors

22:37.600 --> 22:38.900 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
around kind of saying to me,

22:38.900 --> 22:41.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
look, Jason, I think you
could do something great

22:41.700 --> 22:43.333 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
but you're going to have to
do it in a different way.

22:43.333 --> 22:46.100 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
You're going to have to do
it in an unconventional way.

22:46.100 --> 22:47.633 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>While studying
at the university,

22:47.633 --> 22:50.733 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
Jason became fixated
on higher education.

22:50.733 --> 22:52.600 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
He attributes much
of his success

22:52.600 --> 22:54.266 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
to the encouragement he received

22:54.266 --> 22:57.333 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
from his mentor and close
friend Sandro Sandri.

22:57.333 --> 23:01.833 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>I had a lecturer who just
got a PhD and I'd asked them,

23:01.833 --> 23:03.466 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
what's a PhD?

23:03.466 --> 23:05.300 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And they said that it
means I'm a doctor now

23:05.300 --> 23:07.333 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and you just need to
concentrate on studying

23:07.333 --> 23:09.500 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
'cause you won't
have one of these.

23:09.500 --> 23:12.166 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
And I didn't take any I offense,

23:12.166 --> 23:13.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that I thought, yeah,
they were right.

23:13.533 --> 23:15.566 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
I mean, why would I think that?

23:15.566 --> 23:20.633 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And so, that evening, for
some reason, I met Sandro

23:21.666 --> 23:23.133 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and I explained
Sandro what this was,

23:23.133 --> 23:25.500 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and I said to him, can
you imagine if we did one,

23:26.633 --> 23:27.900 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
whatever that means?

23:27.900 --> 23:29.433 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And Sandro kind of
started laughing.

23:29.433 --> 23:31.666 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
I said, what are
you laughing about?

23:31.666 --> 23:32.833 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
You know, and he said,

23:34.200 --> 23:35.633 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
he said, Jason, you know,

23:35.633 --> 23:37.333 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
he paused after
this kind of laugh

23:37.333 --> 23:39.400 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and was straight
face and said to me,

23:39.400 --> 23:44.200 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
how crazy would it be if
you managed to get a PhD?

23:45.000 --> 23:46.300 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
So, at the time I was 22.

23:46.300 --> 23:48.833 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
He said if you got a
PhD in eight years time,

23:48.833 --> 23:49.966 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
you would've been
reading and writing

23:49.966 --> 23:52.233 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
for less than 12 years.

23:52.233 --> 23:54.133 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
Can you imagine if we did that?

23:54.133 --> 23:56.733 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>Jason went on to
acquire two master degrees

23:56.733 --> 23:59.500 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and a PhD in
educational studies.

23:59.500 --> 24:01.133 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
Last March, Jason became

24:01.133 --> 24:03.500 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
the youngest Black
professional ever appointed

24:03.500 --> 24:04.766 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
to a professorship

24:04.766 --> 24:07.666 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
at the prestigious
University of Cambridge.

24:07.666 --> 24:09.866 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
>>We are incredibly proud

24:09.866 --> 24:11.833 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
that Dr. Arday has joined us

24:11.833 --> 24:13.966 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
here in the faculty
of education.

24:13.966 --> 24:15.466 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
Clearly we are very proud

24:15.466 --> 24:18.400 align:left position:10% line:5% size:80%
because he's the
youngest ever professor

24:18.400 --> 24:22.900 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
to be appointed in Oxford or
Cambridge, Black professor.

24:22.900 --> 24:25.733 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And also, because
of the contribution

24:25.733 --> 24:28.033 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that he makes here
to the faculty,

24:28.033 --> 24:31.800 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
the quality of his ideas,
the quality of his research.

24:33.166 --> 24:35.033 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>Jason's goal is
to utilize this platform

24:35.033 --> 24:36.766 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
to affect real change.

24:36.766 --> 24:38.900 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
He aims to open
doors for individuals

24:38.900 --> 24:40.633 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
from disadvantaged backgrounds,

24:40.633 --> 24:42.733 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
democratize higher education,

24:42.733 --> 24:46.433 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and lead the way toward a more
inclusive academic landscape.

24:46.433 --> 24:48.833 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>I think there's such
a breadth of research

24:48.833 --> 24:51.000 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
in the faculty of education

24:51.000 --> 24:53.866 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and I feel really fortunate
to be a small part of that.

24:53.866 --> 24:55.033 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
But I feel even more fortunate

24:55.033 --> 24:56.300 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
because I think
there's an opportunity

24:56.300 --> 24:59.200 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
to learn some really
good practice,

24:59.200 --> 25:03.133 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
you know, world leading
research and some other methods

25:03.133 --> 25:06.833 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and methodological ways of
kind of constructing knowledge

25:06.833 --> 25:08.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that I'm really,
really excited about.

25:08.533 --> 25:10.866 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And probably the
thing that trumps that

25:10.866 --> 25:12.666 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
is just kind of seeing

25:12.666 --> 25:14.533 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
hopefully the number
of Black students

25:14.533 --> 25:17.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
that will continue to
come through these doors.

25:17.700 --> 25:22.766 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And if my appointment has
inspired that in some small way,

25:24.100 --> 25:25.633 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
then that's great and I
think the privilege will be

25:25.633 --> 25:26.866 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
to be able to work with them

25:26.866 --> 25:28.900 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and to be able to
harness their dreams

25:28.900 --> 25:32.233 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
and to show them
everything that I know

25:32.233 --> 25:33.533 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
so they can go on and be

25:33.533 --> 25:35.433 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
as great as they
were destined to be.

25:36.866 --> 25:39.733 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
>>For A World of
Difference, I'm Cindy Peterson.

25:44.800 --> 25:46.633 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
>>Thanks Cindy and Elijah.

25:46.633 --> 25:50.700 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And congratulations Dr. Jason
Arday for making a difference.

25:50.700 --> 25:52.733 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
And that does it
for this edition

25:52.733 --> 25:55.733 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
of A World of Difference
embracing neurodiversity.

25:55.733 --> 25:57.000 align:left position:10% line:89% size:80%
I'm Darryl Owens.

25:57.000 --> 25:58.800 align:left position:10% line:83% size:80%
I'll see you back
here next time.

